ned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5{x}^{3}y+5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-334070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3}y"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}y"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{5}{4}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{3}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-333430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2(2)^{2}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-2(1)^{2}(1)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-334075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-8({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-{y}^{3})+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot 1-0.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.25(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.5}{0.75(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.142\\,857 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.134\\,757 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.134\\,728\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-333426" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-333426" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.13"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+{y}^{2})+({x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $5$ to eliminate the decimals, we obtain\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5\\cdot 0+0+{0}^{2}+0.4 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2({y}_{\\text{new}})-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+0.4}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-5}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=0,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =0.08 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.081\\,322 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.081\\,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈0.08,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-334426" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334426" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.08"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+4{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ starting from $x=0.5,$ we use a step size\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-x+4{y}^{2})+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in this step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+0+0-0.25(0.5)-0.25(1) \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y(0.5)=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}{{g}^{′}(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}^{\\,2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.375}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1}=y(0.5)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-0.375 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-0.29464 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-0.29058 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{5} & ≈-0.29057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈-0.29,$\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-334197" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334197" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.29"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((3x-{y}^{3})+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-1.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-1.5\\cdot 1-1.5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5-1-1.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.179\\,747 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.179\\,509\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.18,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-334469" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334469" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.18"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+{y}^{3},\\,y(1)=-1.5\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+{y}^{3})+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+x+0.5{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+(-1.5)+1+0.5(-1.5)^{3}+2 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.5+1-1.6875+2 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.1875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-1.5$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.1875}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1)=-1.5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-1.5 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-1.342\\,105 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-1.310\\,301 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-1.309\\,019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-1.31,$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334473" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "65f50a505f9ce3456e7211a70d968570308a68bcc57004754eca2e822a539698"} {"question" "q-334473" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "q-334473" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.31"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/state.json" "file_sha256" "5ac140158747f1464abf6af2f63553af74a620f7d11ad2416483fa46e562b707"} {"question" "e-21679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/example-21679.json" "file_sha256" "bb23e3182a02d476b071f192971797df053eb7a2cb6c9abba8bf3ac1a9908154"} {"question" "e-21679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}+2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}} = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-{1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/example-21679.json" "file_sha256" "bb23e3182a02d476b071f192971797df053eb7a2cb6c9abba8bf3ac1a9908154"} {"question" "e-21677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2 - xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/example-21677.json" "file_sha256" "0f3a8c5be1345c38ff05162f9c23cd0f2eb61d991356558e870b4885b84a995b"} {"question" "e-21677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2-x{y}^{2})+(2-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-x{y}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\  & =2-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2-0.5x{y}^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5\\cdot 2\\,({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-2+0.5\\cdot 1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 2(y_\\text{new}) + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1}\n= (y_\\text{new})_n\n- \\dfrac{((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 2.5}\n       {2(y_\\text{new})_n + 1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1) = 1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.166\\,667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.158\\,333 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.158\\,312\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1.16},$ rounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/example-21677.json" "file_sha256" "0f3a8c5be1345c38ff05162f9c23cd0f2eb61d991356558e870b4885b84a995b"} {"question" "q-333430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "ca9e8bc0b41387cae09781c09b8614e1593f98e3206e2dd68caeb7d86a74cbe3" "reviewer_session" "01a118ea-3923-70c2-acd9-0830602bde5f" "rationale" "The trapezoidal increment with h=0.5 is (h/2)[4x²√y+4x_new²√y_new]=x²√y+x_new²√y_new. Substituting into y_new=y+Δy and moving terms left gives g(y_new)=y_new−x_new²√y_new−y−x²√y. Original option c (index 2) and the authentic worked solution agree. Submitted option d replaced √y_new with y_new and was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. The stored formula preserves the same function boundary, subscripts, powers, roots, and signs." "bindings" {"problem" "dbbc35c6e5bbbdcdfebc7b1499bdb6143b49191b9813f2b6b2c23780098d6e3a" "worked_solution" "835856e11c276ca52ca2f346e9788908ec232e55ed05d50b19c9579f49a644f5" "original_field" "7e24ffd5764c69254853ef102b0f4bc3974fe16c851453bdd18f54d3c3a3e569" "reviewed_field" "fbfb2a153c02b4099b50ca3ee61d1bad5a708fe87ed4c0ec03671def64c5c3ca" "raw_widget" "75e9ef711e67a650643642b7685de137e4f7a2e2c0cbb3f7e3f260cae7101650"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-333430-before.json" "sha256" "1767751cfcff756bca827d0ca88471f5ebdad5a3cc79029ae9a6ac3bce0e3452"} {"path" "q-333430-before.png" "sha256" "141bfa5a90b76f44a4a6e851f51e8874a8fb77b2c1d4e7496038cf20c502ec5a"} {"path" "q-333430-after.png" "sha256" "f10a0636ad041f5d08cba6cac10e39eadbca973bb8cdf219b7443427dbb4ee17"} {"path" "history-q-333430.json" "sha256" "935138b7c78bb4274ea0963b22f6708ea4b024cd345190ba7e936bec9982cb05"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "c7adf7edfba8963db112f60c536411dfee58259f611b0999e66c891b70ca871d"}]} {"question" "q-333430" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"] ["math" "g({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-y-2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056690/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "ca9e8bc0b41387cae09781c09b8614e1593f98e3206e2dd68caeb7d86a74cbe3" "rationale" "The trapezoidal increment with h=0.5 is (h/2)[4x²√y+4x_new²√y_new]=x²√y+x_new²√y_new. Substituting into y_new=y+Δy and moving terms left gives g(y_new)=y_new−x_new²√y_new−y−x²√y. Original option c (index 2) and the authentic worked solution agree. Submitted option d replaced √y_new with y_new and was graded Incorrect; this is a derived source-backed judgment. The stored formula preserves the same function boundary, subscripts, powers, roots, and signs."}] "usage" {"e-21679" [] "e-21677" [] "q-333430" [] "q-333426" []} "basis" 1836 "retractions" [[17592186229611 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = 4x^{2}\\sqrt{y}, y(0) = 2$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx = 0.5$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g(y_{new})$ in terms of the new values $x_{new}$ and $y_{new}$, and the current values $x$ and $y$?"] [17592186229611 :question/answer-fields 17592186228101] [17592186229609 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186228861 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$"] [17592186228861 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2y^{2} - \\sqrt{x} + 2(y_{new})^{2} - \\sqrt{x_{new}}) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + y^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} + (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x_{new}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - y - y^2 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x} + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{x_\\text{new}}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - 1 - 1^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{2} - \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228860 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2 - xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places."] [17592186228860 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((2 - xy^{2}) + (2 - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2})) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - xy^{2} - x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 2 - 0.5xy^{2} - 0.5x_{new}(y_{new})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5x_\\text{new}(y_\\text{new})^2 + y_\\text{new} - y - 2 + 0.5xy^2\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5 \\cdot 2(y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 1 - 2 + 0.5 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{2} + y_{new} - 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 2(y_\\text{new}) + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 2.5} {2(y_\\text{new})_n + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1) = 1$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= 1 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 1.166667 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 1.158333 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 1.158312\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{1.16}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228102 17592186228103 17592186228104 17592186228105 17592186228106] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228094 :answer-field/id] [17592186228094 :answer-field/key] [17592186228094 :answer-field/type] [17592186228101 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228101 :answer-field/id] [17592186228101 :answer-field/key] [17592186228101 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
